## What this page is, and what it is not

[Fleet services](/services/fleet-services) is the work: the brakes, the
suspension, the tires, the transmissions, the PM intervals. This page is the
arrangement around it — how the billing runs, how the yard gets scheduled, and
what a maintenance program looks like when it covers twenty units instead of
one.

## National account billing

We accept national account service through Michelin, Bridgestone and Yokohama.
If your tires are already on one of those programs, the paperwork side of a tire
job here works the way it does everywhere else on the network.

## Scheduling a yard rather than a truck

A fleet does not book a repair, it books capacity. Preventative maintenance runs
on the mileage and duty cycle of each unit, DOT inspections run annually per
vehicle, and neither of those is a thing to remember one truck at a time. Tell
us what the fleet looks like — how many units, what they run, where they run it
— and we will put the intervals against a calendar.

## An alignment program, not an alignment

Alignment is the piece with the clearest return, and it is the piece most
programs leave out. Checking geometry at every PM interval and after every tire
or suspension event costs bay time; not checking it costs tread, fuel and
uptime, continuously and invisibly.

{% specs label="Where the money goes" rows=[
  {label: "Tire wear", value: "Shoulder and feather", note: "toe and camber out of specification"},
  {label: "Fuel", value: "~1% MPG", note: "extra rolling resistance from thrust angle"},
  {label: "Downtime", value: "Between PMs", note: "steering and suspension wear early"}
] /%}

We put the arithmetic behind that in [the real ROI of a fleet alignment
program](/articles/how-misalignment-eats-tires-fuel-and-uptime-the-real-roi-of-a-fleet-alignment-program).

